The '''Constitutional Amendment on Sexual Orientation Protections''' ({{lang|ca|Esmena Constitucional sobre les Proteccions per Orientació Sexual}}), colloquially known as the '''Fourteenth Guarantee''', is a {{w|constitutional amendment}} to the [[Twelve Guarantees]] of [[Balboa]] passed by the [[National Assembly of Balboa|National Assembly]] in 2008. The amendment added sexual orientation to the list of protected categories under the Guarantees' anti-discrimination framework, making Balboa one of the first countries in the Abyalan region to constitutionally protect sexual orientation.

== Background ==

Same-sex sexual activity was {{w|decriminalization|decriminalised}} in 1973 as part of the legal harmonisation that accompanied reunification, but no affirmative protections were enacted in the immediate post-reunification period. The 1974 constitution incorporated the [[Twelve Guarantees]] but did not address sexual orientation.

The first {{w|Pride parade|Pride march}} in Balboa was held in the [[Sòtol, Sant Cristòfor|Sòtol]] neighbourhood of [[Sant Cristòfor]] in 1993, marking the beginning of sustained political organising for LGBT rights. The first community health clinic serving LGBT patients opened in Sòtol in 1996. The Civil Partnership Act, passed in 2004, granted registered same-sex couples {{w|civil union|legal recognition}}, {{w|inheritance}} rights, next-of-kin status, and limited {{w|parental rights}}. The Act was introduced by [[Júlia Ballester i Masip]], a [[Balboan People's Party|BPP]] member of the National Assembly representing the Sant Cristòfor–Dàrsena constituency.

Despite these legislative advances, the absence of constitutional protections meant that {{w|discrimination}} on the basis of sexual orientation could only be challenged under general {{w|anti-discrimination law}}, which did not explicitly enumerate sexual orientation as a protected category. The 2006 case ''[[Agramunt v. Balboa Pension Fund]]'', in which a civil partner was denied survivor {{w|pension}} benefits, demonstrated the limitations of the existing framework and underscored the need for constitutional-level protections.

== Campaign ==

The campaign for the amendment was led by a coalition of LGBT organisations, civil liberties groups, and progressive political parties. The [[Sòtol Community Coalition]], a network of LGBT organisations founded by [[Miquel Esteve i Cardona]], coordinated the public campaign, which included lobbying National Assembly members, organising public demonstrations, and publishing advocacy materials.

The campaign emphasised that the [[Constitutional Amendment on Sex-Based Discrimination]] of 1986 had established the precedent that the Guarantees could be expanded to address emerging forms of discrimination. Proponents argued that sexual orientation was an analogous category that required the same level of constitutional protection.

Opposition was led by conservative and religious groups, particularly in [[Tramuntana Province|Tramuntana]] and [[Nurra Province|Nurra]], where the [[Balboan Catholic Church]] maintained significant influence. Opponents argued that the amendment would undermine traditional family structures and that existing civil partnership legislation was sufficient.

== Provisions ==

The amendment added sexual orientation to the protected categories under the [[Twelve Guarantees]] framework. It prohibited {{w|discrimination}} on the basis of sexual orientation in employment, housing, education, healthcare, and access to goods and services. Complaints are adjudicated by the [[National Anti-Discrimination Tribunal]], which has the power to order remedies including compensation, reinstatement, and {{w|injunction}}s.

The amendment did not address {{w|same-sex marriage|marriage equality}}, {{w|adoption}} rights for same-sex couples, or {{w|gender identity}} protections, which were addressed by subsequent legislation: the Marriage Equality Act (2011) and the [[Constitutional Amendment on Gender Identity Protections]] (2019).

== Text ==

The amendment reads as follows:

{{Quote|'''Fourteenth Guarantee''' — No person shall be subjected to discrimination on the basis of sexual orientation in any sphere of public life, including employment, housing, education, healthcare, and the provision of goods and services. The protections afforded under this Guarantee shall apply equally to all persons regardless of their sexual orientation, and the National Anti-Discrimination Tribunal shall have jurisdiction to adjudicate complaints arising under this Guarantee.}}

== Passage ==

The amendment was introduced in the National Assembly by [[Júlia Ballester i Masip]], who had previously introduced the Civil Partnership Act in 2004. The amendment passed with the required {{w|supermajority}} in the National Assembly and was ratified by [[Adonian Community]] review. The [[Balboan People's Party|BPP]] government supported the amendment, with additional support from the [[Liberal Party of Balboa|Liberal Party]] and the [[Socialist Front of Balboa]].

== Legacy ==

The amendment was the second expansion of the Twelve Guarantees, following the [[Constitutional Amendment on Sex-Based Discrimination]] of 1986. It established constitutional protections for sexual orientation that were later cited in the campaign for the Marriage Equality Act (2011), which extended full marriage rights to same-sex couples.

The amendment was followed by the [[Constitutional Amendment on Gender Identity Protections]] (2019), which added gender identity to the protected categories and completed the constitutional framework for LGBT rights in Balboa.

